---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SalaryCalculator from '@/components/tools/finance/SalaryCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="salary-calculator">
  <SalaryCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the salary calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Enter the <strong>pay amount</strong> you know and pick its period: hourly, daily, weekly,
        bi-weekly, semi-monthly, monthly, quarterly or annual.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set your <strong>hours per week</strong>, <strong>days per week</strong> and
        <strong>weeks per year</strong>. The defaults give a standard full-time year of 2,080 hours.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter your <strong>public holidays</strong> and <strong>vacation days</strong> to see the adjusted
        column, which treats that time as unpaid.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open <strong>raise mode</strong> to see what a percentage increase or a flat annual increase is
        worth in each pay packet.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The salary conversions</h2>
    <p>
      All conversions go through the annual figure. From an hourly rate:
      <code>annual = hourly × hours per week × weeks per year</code>. Going the other way:
      <code>hourly = annual / (hours per week × weeks per year)</code>. A standard full-time year is
      40 × 52 = 2,080 hours, so a salary divided by 2,080 gives a usable hourly rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bi-weekly and semi-monthly count pay dates, not weeks, so they do not match: 26 bi-weekly payments a
      year against 24 semi-monthly ones. Semi-monthly payments are larger. Bi-weekly payments come more
      often, and some calendar years have 27 of them.
    </p>

