          <tr><td>90,000</td><td style="text-align:right">43.27</td><td style="text-align:right">3,462</td><td style="text-align:right">7,500</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100,000</td><td style="text-align:right">48.08</td><td style="text-align:right">3,846</td><td style="text-align:right">8,333</td></tr>
          <tr><td>120,000</td><td style="text-align:right">57.69</td><td style="text-align:right">4,615</td><td style="text-align:right">10,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>150,000</td><td style="text-align:right">72.12</td><td style="text-align:right">5,769</td><td style="text-align:right">12,500</td></tr>
          <tr><td>200,000</td><td style="text-align:right">96.15</td><td style="text-align:right">7,692</td><td style="text-align:right">16,667</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Comparing an hourly job with a salaried one</h2>
    <p>
      A salaried role usually includes paid public holidays and paid leave; a contract or hourly role often
      does not. A comparison of the headline rates therefore makes the hourly job look better than it is.
      With 10 public holidays and 15 days of leave, an hourly worker on a five-day week loses five weeks of
      pay a year: 47 paid weeks in place of 52, or about 9.6% less income for the same weekly hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      The adjusted column in the calculator makes this comparison. Contractors often add 20–30% to an
      equivalent salary rate to cover unpaid leave, sick days, employer pension contributions and the
      time between contracts.
    </p>

    <h2>What a raise is worth</h2>
    <p>
      Raise mode converts a percentage into money per hour, per week and per pay packet. A raise below the
      inflation rate is a pay cut in real terms; the inflation calculator gives the current rate. A raise
      also compounds, because next year's increase applies to the higher base.
    </p>

    <h2>These are gross figures</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator deducts nothing. Income tax, social security or national insurance, pension
      contributions, health insurance premiums and student loan repayments come out of these amounts, at
      rates that depend on where you live and your personal circumstances. Use this tool to compare offers
      and convert between pay periods. For take-home pay, use your local tax authority's calculator.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
